{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015T4AJABBoog3DDLCWUBmzW","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Checklist for `true-bdd scen check [scenario-id...]`.\n2\t#\n3\t# One cell per (scenario, prompt): each prompt reads ONE registry entry\n4\t# and rules on it alone. scen check is the verifier: a bare run exits 1\n5\t# when any scenario fails a prompt, so it gates a change, and every\n6\t# prompt carries an `F:`, so `--fix` walks the whole checklist.\n7\t#\n8\t# term_closure holds a scenario's words to the documents that define\n9\t# them. Its four `D:` prompts are answered by the engine's own checks,\n10\t# with no AI turn, before any `Q:`; a scenario failing one skips its\n11\t# `Q:` prompts until it is fixed. Their fixes write the product or the\n12\t# architecture document, never the scenario. Its two `Q:` prompts ask a\n13\t# model what no check can settle: whether a phrase's meaning says\n14\t# something, and whether the scenario uses the phrase in that meaning.\n15\t# Every closure prompt passes a scenario no Story claims, or a host\n16\t# whose product document declares no catalogue, as \"not checked\".\n17\t#\n18\t# scenario_quality holds the eight questions of the `us-refine`\n19\t# acceptance-criteria checklist that still mean something applied to a\n20\t# single scenario; its fixes rewrite the scenario's own registry entry.\n21\t# The four it dropped threshold over a set (AC count 3-7, at least one\n22\t# happy path, at least two error scenarios, at least 80% convertible).\n23\t\n24\tversion: \"1.0\"\n25\tlast_updated: \"2026-10-21\"\n26\tsections:\n27\t    - id: scenario_quality\n28\t      name: \"Scenario Quality\"\n29\t      description: \"Every registry scenario must read as one testable behaviour, in the product's vocabulary.\"\n30\t      validation_prompts:\n31\t        - Q: |\n32\t            Decide whether this scenario includes at least one specific\n33\t            measurable criterion — a status code, an exact message, a\n34\t            timeout, an error code, a named field, a count.\n35\t\n36\t            Pass when at least one such criterion appears in the description\n37\t            or in a step.\n38\t            Fail when none does.\n39\t\n40\t            Context: emit one line.\n41\t              - \"<scenario id>: measurable — '<criterion>'\"\n42\t              - \"<scenario id>: not measurable\"\n43\t          rationale: \"A measurable criterion is what a step definition asserts on; without one the test can only re-state the prose.\"\n44\t          F: \"Add one measurable criterion (an exact message, a count, a code or a threshold) to the step of this Scenario's registry entry that asserts its outcome, asking which criterion first. Keep the behaviour, and change no other step.\"\n45\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d980a524-959d-4997-9b9c-887b534d1487","uuid":"fd4bfb7e-66a6-484b-9d5d-ce8b4b8b847d","timestamp":"2026-10-21T20:11:55.907Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"{{CWD}}/true-bdd/checklists/scen-check.yaml","content":"# Checklist for `true-bdd scen check [scenario-id...]`.\n#\n# One cell per (scenario, prompt): each prompt reads ONE registry entry\n# and rules on it alone. scen check is the verifier: a bare run exits 1\n# when any scenario fails a prompt, so it gates a change, and every\n# prompt carries an `F:`, so `--fix` walks the whole checklist.\n#\n# term_closure holds a scenario's words to the documents that define\n# them. Its four `D:` prompts are answered by the engine's own checks,\n# with no AI turn, before any `Q:`; a scenario failing one skips its\n# `Q:` prompts until it is fixed. Their fixes write the product or the\n# architecture document, never the scenario. Its two `Q:` prompts ask a\n# model what no check can settle: whether a phrase's meaning says\n# something, and whether the scenario uses the phrase in that meaning.\n# Every closure prompt passes a scenario no Story claims, or a host\n# whose product document declares no catalogue, as \"not checked\".\n#\n# scenario_quality holds the eight questions of the `us-refine`\n# acceptance-criteria checklist that still mean something applied to a\n# single scenario; its fixes rewrite the scenario's own registry entry.\n# The four it dropped threshold over a set (AC count 3-7, at least one\n# happy path, at least two error scenarios, at least 80% convertible).\n\nversion: \"1.0\"\nlast_updated: \"2026-10-21\"\nsections:\n    - id: scenario_quality\n      name: \"Scenario Quality\"\n      description: \"Every registry scenario must read as one testable behaviour, in the product's vocabulary.\"\n      validation_prompts:\n        - Q: |\n            Decide whether this scenario includes at least one specific\n            measurable criterion — a status code, an exact message, a\n            timeout, an error code, a named field, a count.\n\n            Pass when at least one such criterion appears in the description\n            or in a step.\n            Fail when none does.\n\n            Context: emit one line.\n              - \"<scenario id>: measurable — '<criterion>'\"\n              - \"<scenario id>: not measurable\"\n          rationale: \"A measurable criterion is what a step definition asserts on; without one the test can only re-state the prose.\"\n          F: \"Add one measurable criterion (an exact message, a count, a code or a threshold) to the step of this Scenario's registry entry that asserts its outcome, asking which criterion first. Keep the behaviour, and change no other step.\"\n","numLines":45,"startLine":1,"totalLines":45}}}
