as the glossary spells it; the list may be empty.\n244\t\n245\tEvery step of a Scenario a Story claims must match exactly one template\n246\tin the whole catalogue, and that entry's block must be the step's block.\n247\tA step opening with `llm:` or `judge:` is run by a model and exempt.\n248\t\n249\tTemplate grammar, token by token:\n250\t\n251\t- literal text: itself, exactly, case and whitespace included.\n252\t- `\"{name}\"`: one or more characters other than `\"`; the slot sits\n253\t  between literal double quotes.\n254\t- `{name:number}`: digits, or the word `no` for zero.\n255\t- `{name:role}`: exactly one `roles[].name` of the product document.\n256\t- `{name:pattern}`: the rest of the step, verbatim; only as the\n257\t  template's last token, after a space.\n258\t- `[a|b]`: exactly one of the literal alternatives.\n259\t- `[s]`: `s`, or nothing.\n260\t- `\\{`, `\\}`, `\\[`, `\\]`, `\\\\`: the literal character.\n261\t\n262\tSlot names are lower-case letters and underscores, unique within a\n263\ttemplate. A template matches a step's whole text, never a part of it.\n264\t\n265\tA good entry phrases the step in the shape other Scenarios will reuse:\n266\tquoted runs and numbers become slots, the words that carry the meaning\n267\tstay literal. It must not also match a step another entry already\n268\tphrases, or that step becomes ambiguous.\n269\t{{- if eq .FailedCheck.Check \"declared-terms\" }}\n270\t\n271\t## The glossary\n272\t\n273\tThe product document's `glossary:` list defines the words catalogue\n274\tentries declare in their `terms`. A glossary entry has two fields:\n275\t\n276\t- `term`: the word, spelled exactly as the catalogue entries declaring\n277\t  it spell it, case and spacing included. A term is looked up by that\n278\t  spelling and nothing else.\n279\t- `means`: what the word is in this product and why it matters to a\n280\t  reader of a Scenario, in product words. It says what the word means,\n281\t  never how the code implements it.\n282\t\n283\tEvery term a catalogue entry declares, through a step that matches\n284\tthat entry, must be defined exactly once. A term defined twice has two\n285\tmeanings, so a reader can rely on neither.\n286\t\n287\tA good definition reads true in every Scenario whose phrases declare\n288\tthe term, not only in this one: read the `means` of each catalogue\n289\tentry declaring it before you write it.\n290\t{{- else if eq .FailedCheck.Check \"term-mappings\" }}\n291\t\n292\t## The word mapping\n293\t\n294\tThe architecture document's `architecture.terms:` list maps each\n295\tproduct word the catalogue entries declare in their `terms` onto the\n296\timplementation. A mapping has three fields:\n297\t\n298\t- `term`: the word, spelled exactly as the catalogue entries declaring\n299\t  it spell it, case and spacing included. A term is looked up by that\n300\t  spelling and nothing else.\n301\t- `technical`: what the word is in the implementation: the process,\n302\t  request, record, file or field that carries it, named in words.\n303\t- `observed_by`: how a step definition sets the word up or observes\n304\t  it, in words a reader can follow to write that definition: what it\n305\t  starts, sends or reads, and what it compares. It never names a code\n306\t  identifier, so the definition can be rebuilt from it.\n307\t\n308\tEvery term a catalogue entry declares, through a step that matches\n309\tthat entry, must be mapped exactly once. A term mapped twice is built\n310\ttwo ways, so no step definition can follow both.\n311\t\n312\tA good mapping holds for every Scenario whose phrases declare the\n313\tterm, not only for this one: read the `means` of each catalogue entry\n314\tdeclaring it, the term's glossary entry, and the mappings already in\n315\tthe architecture document before you write it, and say the same kind\n316\tof thing the existing mappings say.\n317\t{{- else if eq .FailedCheck.Check \"step-definitions\" }}\n318\t\n319\t## The step definitions\n320\t\n32